  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "🍄 Gnome Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Personal name, clan name and nickname: the three names a gnome picks because they're the most fun to say."
    },
    "intro": "Gnomes collect names the way other people collect buttons. In D&D a gnome gets a name from their mother, their father, a clan elder and assorted aunts and uncles, plus nicknames from everyone else, and ends up with half a dozen or more. With outsiders they settle on three. This **gnome name generator** gives you those three: a personal name, a clan name and a nickname, like Fizwick “Ticktock” Spindleworth.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Why gnomes have so many names",
        "content": "<p>The fifth-edition Player's Handbook (2014) says gnomes love names. Each relative gives a newborn gnome a name, usually a variation on the name of an ancestor or distant relative, though some are brand new inventions. Nicknames from everyone else may or may not stick.</p><p>Other peoples find this exhausting, so when dealing with humans and others who are \"stuffy\" about names, a gnome uses no more than three: a <strong>personal name</strong>, a <strong>clan name</strong> and a <strong>nickname</strong>, picking the one in each category that is the most fun to say. That last rule is the best guide to a good gnome name.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "What gnome names sound like",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Personal names bounce.</strong> Doubled consonants and light endings: <em>-bble</em>, <em>-wick</em>, <em>-bin</em>, <em>-bel</em>, <em>-mip</em>. Say Tibble, Lillibee or Quibbin and it's hard not to smile.</li><li><strong>Clan names sound like a family workshop:</strong> Copperkettle, Spindleworth, Tinkerton. Rock gnomes, D&D's tinkerers, suit the clockwork-sounding ones; forest gnomes suit Mossworth or Jinglebrook.</li><li><strong>Nicknames are short and earned:</strong> Kaboom, Sootface, Twofingers. For a rock gnome inventor, the nickname is often a warning.</li></ul><p>All the names here are new, built from those sounds rather than copied from the Player's Handbook.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Giving your gnome more names",
        "content": "<p>The three-name format is the polite version. At the table it's fun to let the rest slip out:</p><ul><li><strong>Give the full list once.</strong> When the gnome first introduces themselves to someone important, let them rattle off six names, then say \"but you can call me Pemble.\"</li><li><strong>Change the nickname.</strong> Nicknames come and go. A gnome who blew up the party's cart can arrive at the next town as \"Kaboom\".</li><li><strong>Ask who gave which name.</strong> A name from a disapproving aunt is a story in itself.</li></ul><p>Press Generate a few times with \"Personal name only\" to collect a whole family's worth of names.</p>"
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "How many names does a gnome have in D&D?",
        "answer": "Usually half a dozen or more, given by parents, the clan elder, aunts, uncles and friends. With outsiders, a gnome uses three: a personal name, a clan name and a nickname."
      },
      {
        "question": "What's the difference between rock gnome and forest gnome names?",
        "answer": "The Player's Handbook gives both the same kind of name. For flavour, rock gnome tinkerers suit workshop-sounding clan names like Copperkettle, and forest gnomes suit names from nature, like Mossworth."
      },
      {
        "question": "Are these names from the Player's Handbook?",
        "answer": "No. They're new names built in the same style, so your gnome won't share a name with an example from the book."
      },
      {
        "question": "Do deep gnomes use the same names?",
        "answer": "Deep gnomes, or svirfneblin, are a separate people who live in the Underdark, with their own culture. These bouncy names suit rock and forest gnomes best; for a deep gnome, pick the plainer ones."
      }
    ]
  }
}
